@node Overview
@chapter Overview

Twist is a transfer library and a small command-line client.  The
public interface is @code{twist_easy}, @code{twist_multi} and
@code{twist_share}.  It is not a drop-in replacement for the
@code{libcurl} ABI: names, constants and header files are Twist's own.

HTTP is the scheme that is implemented.  Every other URL scheme that
libcurl offers has a row in the scheme table, so a later handler can
be added without changing the transfer engine.  TLS and QUIC come from
GNU Quickstep.  The reactor does not start a thread of its own.  A
transfer's sockets stay on one loop, and QUIC is one UDP socket on
that same loop.

@node Library
@chapter Library

Call @code{twist_global_init} before any other function, and
@code{twist_global_cleanup} when the process is finished with the
library.

An easy handle is created with @code{twist_easy_init}, configured with
@code{twist_easy_setopt}, run with @code{twist_easy_perform} and
released with @code{twist_easy_cleanup}.  Several easy handles can
share one loop through @code{twist_multi_init} and
@code{twist_multi_socket_action}.  @code{twist_multi_timeout} is the
number of milliseconds until the next timer, or @minus{}1 when nothing
is scheduled.

@node Invocation
@chapter Invocation

@example
twist [OPTION]... URL...
@end example

The body is written to standard output, or to the file named by
@option{-o} or @option{-O}.  Diagnostics are written to standard
error, or to @option{--stderr}.  @option{--help} and @option{--version}
are written to standard output and exit successfully.

A transfer error has the form @samp{twist: (@var{n}) @var{message}} and
exits @var{n}, using curl's status numbers.  A bad command line exits
2, with no number in parentheses, and suggests @option{--help}.
@option{-s} hides a transfer diagnostic.  @option{-S} shows it again.
Several URLs leave the status of the last transfer, unless
@option{--fail-early} stops at the first failure.

@table @option
@item -h
@itemx --help
Print a help message on standard output and exit successfully.
@item --version
Print version and licensing information on standard output and exit
successfully.
@item -o @var{file}
@itemx --output @var{file}
Write the response body to @var{file}.  @code{-} is standard output.
@item -O
@itemx --remote-name
Write the body to the last segment of the URL path.
@item --output-dir @var{dir}
Directory for a relative @option{-o} or @option{-O} name.  Reset by
@option{--next}.
@item --create-dirs
Create missing parent directories for @option{-o} and @option{-O}.
Reset by @option{--next}.
@item -X @var{method}
@itemx --request @var{method}
Request method.
@item -H @var{line}
@itemx --header @var{line}
Extra request header.  Repeat for each header.
@item -d @var{data}
@itemx --data @var{data}
Request body.  A leading @code{@@} reads a file, and @code{@@-} reads
standard input.  Carriage returns and line feeds are removed.
Repeated @option{-d} values are joined with @code{&}.
@item --data-binary @var{data}
Request body, kept as given, including a file read with @code{@@}.
@item --data-raw @var{data}
Request body.  @code{@@} is not a file.
@item --data-urlencode @var{data}
Form field.  The value is percent-encoded.  @code{name=value} encodes
the value, @code{name@@file} encodes the file, and @code{@@file} encodes
the file with no name.
@item -G
@itemx --get
Append @option{-d} to the query string instead of sending a request
body.
@item -T @var{file}
@itemx --upload-file @var{file}
Upload @var{file} with PUT.  @code{-} reads standard input.  For
@code{sftp} and @code{scp} the file is sent as it is read, so a large file or
a pipe is not held in memory.  For other schemes it is read before the
transfer starts.
@item -I
@itemx --head
Fetch headers only.
@item -i
@itemx --include
Write response headers before the body.
@item -D @var{file}
@itemx --dump-header @var{file}
Write response headers to @var{file}.  @code{-} is standard output.
@item -L
@itemx --location
Follow redirections.
@item --max-redirs @var{n}
Redirect limit.  The default is 50.
@item -f
@itemx --fail
Fail the transfer when the HTTP status is 400 or above, and do not
write the body.  Without this option a 404 is a successful transfer:
the status is recorded and the body is written.
@item --fail-early
Stop at the first failed URL.  This applies to every URL on the
command line.
@item --compressed
Ask for a gzip or deflate response and decompress it.
@item --content-encoding @var{coding}
Compress the request body.  @var{coding} is @code{gzip} or
@code{deflate}.  The request carries @code{Content-Encoding}.  Reset
by @option{--next}.
@item -k
@itemx --insecure
Accepted for compatibility with curl.  The TLS engine still checks the
configured trust store.  For @code{sftp} and @code{scp} it skips the
@file{known_hosts} check (@pxref{SFTP and SCP}); a fingerprint given with
@option{--hostpubsha256} is still checked.
@item -x @var{url}
@itemx --proxy @var{url}
Proxy.  Use @code{http://} or @code{socks5://} (@code{socks5h://} is the same),
with a port (the default is 8080 for HTTP and 1080 for SOCKS) and, if the proxy wants them, @samp{user:password@@} before the host,
percent-encoded.  An IPv6 proxy is in brackets.  @code{https://} and
@code{socks4://} proxies are refused.  For @code{sftp} and @code{scp}
(@pxref{SFTP and SCP}) the proxy opens a tunnel.
@item -U @var{user:password}
@itemx --proxy-user @var{user:password}
The user name and password for the proxy, if its URL has none.
@item -A @var{name}
@itemx --user-agent @var{name}
User-Agent header.
@item -u @var{user[:password]}
@itemx --user @var{user[:password]}
User and password.  Without a colon, the password is read from
@file{/dev/tty} with echo disabled, so a piped standard input is left
for @option{-d} or @option{-T}.
@item -e @var{url}
@itemx --referer @var{url}
Referer header.
@item -r @var{range}
@itemx --range @var{range}
Byte range.  For @code{sftp} and @code{scp} it is @samp{@var{first}-@var{last}},
@samp{@var{first}-}, or @samp{-@var{count}} for the last @var{count} bytes
(@pxref{SFTP and SCP}).
@item -l
@itemx --list-only
List the names only, one to a line, not the server's long form, when the URL
names a directory.  For @code{sftp} (@pxref{SFTP and SCP}).
@item -Q @var{command}
@itemx --quote @var{command}
Run an SFTP command on the server before the transfer, or after a successful
one if @var{command} begins with @samp{-}.  A @samp{*} before it lets the
server refuse it without ending the transfer.  May be given many times.  For
@code{sftp} URLs only; other URLs ignore it (@pxref{SFTP and SCP}).
@item --limit-rate @var{speed}
Keep to @var{speed} bytes a second, for downloads and uploads, for
@code{sftp} and @code{scp}.  The speed is a whole number with an optional
suffix: @samp{k}, @samp{m}, @samp{g}, @samp{t} or @samp{p}, in either case,
each 1024 times the one before (@samp{200K}, @samp{3m}).  @samp{0} is no
limit.  Other kinds of URL do not keep to a rate yet, and are refused with
status 2 rather than ignoring the limit.
@item --ftp-create-dirs
Make the directories an @code{sftp} upload needs, with mode 0755.
@item -C @var{offset}
@itemx --continue-at @var{offset}
Resume at byte @var{offset}, for @code{sftp} and @code{scp}.  Use @samp{-} to
find the offset.  A download continues from the end of the file named by
@option{-o} or @option{-O}, which is opened to add to it; with a number the
file is added to from that byte of the remote file.  An upload with
@option{-T} skips that many bytes of the local file and adds to the remote
one at that offset; with @samp{-} the server is asked how big the remote file
is.  Exits 36 if the remote file is shorter than the offset.
@item -b @var{data}
@itemx --cookie @var{data}
Cookie request header.  This is not a cookie jar file.
@item --oauth2-bearer @var{token}
Bearer token.
@item -m @var{seconds}
@itemx --max-time @var{seconds}
Limit for the whole transfer.  Up to three decimal places are
accepted.
@item --connect-timeout @var{seconds}
Limit for the connect, in seconds.
@item -s
@itemx --silent
Hide the transfer diagnostic.  The exit status is unchanged.
@item -S
@itemx --show-error
Show the transfer diagnostic when @option{-s} was also given.  The
last of @option{-s} and @option{-S} wins.
@item --stderr @var{file}
Write diagnostics to @var{file}.  @code{-} keeps standard error.  This
applies to every URL.
@item -w @var{format}
@itemx --write-out @var{format}
Print @var{format} on standard output after the body.
@code{@{http_code@}}, @code{@{response_code@}},
@code{@{size_download@}}, @code{@{url_effective@}},
@code{@{content_type@}}, @code{@{exitcode@}}, and @code{@{errormsg@}}
are replaced.  @code{\n}, @code{\r}, @code{\t}, and @code{%%} are
recognised.  An unknown @code{@{name@}} is empty.
@item -g
@itemx --globoff
Accepted.  URLs are never expanded.
@item -q
@itemx --disable
Accepted.  No config file is read.
@item --next
Start a separate transfer for the following URL.  Options after it do
not inherit the previous URL's method, body, headers, output, proxy,
user, or timeouts.  @option{-s}, @option{-S}, @option{--stderr}, and
@option{--fail-early} still apply.  A URL is required before
@option{--next}.
@item -E @var{file}
@itemx --cert @var{file}
Client certificate in PEM form, leaf first.  Further certificates in
the file are sent after the leaf.  The matching private key is
@option{--key}, an unencrypted PEM file.  When a server asks for a
client certificate and none was given, the handshake fails.  When one
was given, it is presented.
@item --key @var{file}
Private key for @option{--cert}.  For @code{sftp} and @code{scp} it is the
private key to log in with, and it is used alone: an ssh-agent is not asked
(@pxref{SFTP and SCP}).
@item --pass @var{phrase}
Passphrase for the private key file, for the formats twist can decrypt
(@pxref{SFTP and SCP}).
@item --hostpubsha256 @var{hash}
The SHA-256 of the host key of an @code{sftp} or @code{scp} server, in base64
as @command{ssh-keygen -l} prints it, with or without @samp{SHA256:}.  A host
key that matches is trusted, whatever @file{known_hosts} says; one that does
not is refused, even with @option{-k}.
@item --cacert @var{file}
PEM trust bundle used to verify the peer.
@item --http1.0
@itemx --http1.1
@itemx --http2
@itemx --http2-prior-knowledge
@itemx --http3
@itemx --http3-only
Select the HTTP version.  HTTP/3 is used when discovered from an HTTPS
record or a cached Alt-Svc entry.  HTTP/2 on HTTPS is ALPN.  Cleartext
HTTP/2 is an h2c upgrade, unless prior knowledge was requested.
@item --dns-order @var{list}
Comma-separated DNS transports.  The default is @code{doq,dot,doh,udp}.
DoQ and DoT are used when the system resolver advertises them, or when
@option{--doq-url} or @option{--dot-url} names a server.  UDP retries
over TCP when the answer is truncated.  A host that is an IPv4 or IPv6
address is used as that address.
@item --doh-url @var{url}
Resolve host names through this DNS-over-HTTPS server instead of
@option{--dns-order}.  The URL must use HTTPS.  The server's name is
resolved with DoQ, DoT, or UDP, and not with DoH.  Each query is then an
HTTP POST whose body is one DNS message
(@code{application/dns-message}).  An empty URL
restores the normal resolver.  When @option{--dns-order} is also set,
that list is used and this URL supplies the @code{doh} step.
@item --doh-insecure
Do not verify the DoH server certificate.  This is independent of
@option{-k}.  Curl's @option{--doh-cert-status} is not supported.
@item --doq-url @var{url}
DNS-over-QUIC server.  @code{quic://host} or @code{host}, with an
optional port (default 853).  The certificate is checked against that
host.  The host name is resolved with UDP.  An empty URL clears it.
Without this option, DoQ uses the address and name from a
@code{_dns.resolver.arpa} SVCB record (DDR).
@item --dot-url @var{url}
DNS-over-TLS server.  @code{tls://host} or @code{host}, with an optional
port (default 853).  Same discovery rules as @option{--doq-url}.
@item --mail-from @var{address}
@itemx --mail-rcpt @var{address}
SMTP envelope.  Repeat @option{--mail-rcpt} for each recipient.
@end table

@code{mqtt://host/topic} subscribes to the topic, or publishes
@option{--data} and disconnects.  @code{ldap://host/dn} binds, searches,
and writes the result as LDIF.  @code{ws://host/path} does the HTTP/1
upgrade, or an extended CONNECT on HTTP/2 or HTTP/3, and writes payload
bytes.  An HTTP/1 status other than 101 fails.  @code{gopher://host/type}
sends the selector and writes the reply until the server closes.
@code{dict://host/d:word:database} defines a word,
@code{dict://host/m:word:database:strategy} matches one, and
@code{dict://host/show:db} asks the server for its database list.  The
reply is written until the server closes.
@code{tftp://host/file} reads a file.  @option{--data} or @option{-T}
uploads one.  The transfer offers block size 512 and retransmits a
lost packet.  A proxy is not used.  A server error packet exits 68
through 74.

There is no progress meter, config file, URL globbing,
@option{--fail-with-body}, verbose trace, cookie jar, or parallel
transfer.

@heading Exit status
@table @asis
@item 0
The transfer finished.
@item 1
The scheme is not implemented.
@item 2
The command line is wrong, or twist could not initialise.
@item 3
The URL is malformed.
@item 6
The host name could not be resolved.
@item 7
The connection failed.
@item 8
The server's reply could not be used.
@item 22
@option{--fail} and the HTTP status is 400 or above.
@item 23
The output could not be written.
@item 26
A local file named by @option{-d}, @option{--data-binary},
@option{--data-urlencode}, or @option{-T} could not be read.
@item 27
Out of memory.
@item 25
An @code{sftp} or @code{scp} upload failed.
@item 28
The transfer timed out.
@item 33
The range cannot be delivered: it starts beyond the end of the remote file, or
is not one twist understands.
@item 36
The transfer cannot be resumed: the remote file is shorter than the offset, the
local data is, or the file to resume is not there.
@item 35
The TLS handshake failed.
@item 37
A file URL could not be opened.
@item 42
The transfer was aborted.
@item 43
A bad argument was passed to the library.
@item 47
Too many redirects.
@item 52
The server closed before sending any data.
@item 61
The content encoding could not be decoded.
@item 9
The SFTP server would not let the user read the file.
@item 60
The host key of an @code{sftp} or @code{scp} server is not trusted: it is
not in @file{known_hosts}, it differs from the one there, or it does not
match @option{--hostpubsha256}.
@item 67
Authentication failed.
@item 68
TFTP file not found.
@item 69
TFTP access violation.
@item 70
TFTP disk full.
@item 71
TFTP illegal operation.
@item 72
TFTP unknown transfer.
@item 73
TFTP file already exists.
@item 74
TFTP no such user.
@item 78
The file does not exist on the @code{sftp} or @code{scp} server.
@item 79
The @code{sftp} or @code{scp} server could not do what was asked.
@item 81
The operation could not be started.
@end table

@node SFTP and SCP
@chapter SFTP and SCP

@example
twist sftp://user@@host/path/to/file
twist scp://user@@host/~/file
@end example

twist transfers one file over SSH.  @code{scp://} and @code{sftp://} are the
same transfer, which uses the SFTP subsystem of the server: twist does not
implement the old SCP protocol, as OpenSSH's own @command{scp} has not since
version 9.0.  A server with no SFTP subsystem fails with an error that says
so.  The path is absolute, except that @samp{/~/} begins in the directory
the server puts the user in.  An @code{scp} URL does not expand wildcards.

@subheading Directory listings

An @code{sftp} URL that ends in @samp{/}, or has no path, lists that
directory: @samp{sftp://host/} the root, @samp{sftp://host/~/} the user's home
directory, @samp{sftp://host/dir/} a directory.  Each entry is a line, in
the long form the server gives (like @command{ls -l}), in the order the server
sends them, including @file{.}, @file{..} and files whose names begin with a
dot.  @option{-l} gives the names only.

What a server sends is not trusted with the output: a newline in a file name
would make a line that is not an entry, and control characters can do as they
please to a terminal.  So control characters in a listing, and the UTF-8 form
of the C1 ones, are replaced with @samp{?}.  curl writes them as they come.

An @code{scp} URL does not list: it names one file, and a URL that ends in
@samp{/} exits 78.  A directory that is not there, or a file followed by a
slash, exits 78 too, and one that may not be listed exits 9.  A range or
@option{-C} means nothing for a listing and is refused.

@subheading Upload, resume and ranges

@option{-T @var{file}} uploads a file.  The remote file is created, with mode
0644 as curl does, and truncated if it is there.  When the URL names a
directory (it ends in @samp{/}) the file gets the name of the local file, as
with curl.  Data given with @option{-d} is uploaded the same way.  If the
file cannot be read part way, the transfer fails with status 26; the remote
file is left as far as it got, as with curl.  A pipe is read as it delivers
data, and twist does not do anything else while it waits for the next of it.

@option{-C} continues a transfer that stopped.  An upload does not send what
the server has, and writes on at that offset of the remote file without
truncating it.  A download is added to the end of the output file.

@option{-r} downloads part of a file: @samp{100-199} (both ends included),
@samp{100-} (to the end), or @samp{-50} (the last 50 bytes).  A range that
starts beyond the end of the file exits 33.  A range and @option{-C} cannot
be used together.

The size of the remote file is asked for only when it is needed: to check a
download resume, to find the start of @samp{-50}, or to resume an upload.
A server that does not say how big a file is cannot do the last, nor
@samp{-50}.

@subheading Through a proxy

@option{-x} opens a tunnel to the SSH server through an HTTP proxy
(@code{CONNECT}) or a SOCKS5 proxy, and the SSH session goes through it.
Credentials in the proxy's URL, or given with @option{-U}, are sent as Basic
for HTTP and as a user name and password for SOCKS5.  A refusal is told in
curl's words and with its status: 56 for a @code{CONNECT} that failed
(@samp{CONNECT tunnel failed, response 403}), 97 for SOCKS5 (@samp{Can't
complete SOCKS5 connection to host. (5)}); a proxy that is not there is 7.
The server's banner may arrive in the same packet as the proxy's reply, and
a proxy may say a good deal before it opens the tunnel; both are handled.

The server is named to the proxy as the URL gives it: a name is sent as a name,
and an IP address as an address (an IPv6 one in brackets for @code{CONNECT}).
@code{socks5://} and @code{socks5h://} are the same: twist does not look the
name up itself before giving it to a SOCKS5 proxy, as curl does for
@code{socks5://}.  The tunnel is kept for the next URL as a connection is, but
only for the same proxy with the same credentials: a login made through one
proxy is not used by a transfer through another, or through none.

@subheading IPv6 addresses

An IPv6 address is written in brackets, @samp{sftp://user@@[2001:db8::7]:2222/f}.
known_hosts names it as @command{ssh} does: bare for port 22
(@samp{2001:db8::7}), and in brackets with the port for any other
(@samp{[2001:db8::7]:2222}).  A link-local address names the interface it is
reached by after a percent sign, which a URL writes encoded:
@samp{[fe80::1%25eth0]} (a bare @samp{%eth0} is taken too).  known_hosts keeps
the zone, @samp{fe80::1%eth0}, as @command{ssh} does, and the connection goes
out by that interface; without it a link-local address goes nowhere.  An
interface may be given by number as well.  What is between brackets must be
an IPv6 address: anything else is a malformed URL (status 3), and an interface
that this machine does not have is a connection that cannot be made (status
7).  Neither waits for a lookup.  The effective URL (@samp{%@{url_effective@}})
keeps the brackets and the zone, and an HTTP request names the host in its
@code{Host} header with the brackets and without the zone.

@subheading Commands and missing directories

@option{-Q @var{command}} runs a command on the server before the transfer,
and @option{-Q -@var{command}} after it, if it succeeded.  An @code{sftp} URL
takes them; an @code{scp} URL, like any other kind, ignores them.  They run
one at a time, in the order given, and the first the server refuses ends the
transfer with status 21 and a message that says which command and why.  A
@samp{*} before a command (@samp{-Q '*rm old'}) lets the server refuse it.  A
command twist cannot read is an error even then.  A @samp{+} before a command
is refused: it is the form for FTP.

The commands are the ones the curl tool has.  The command is the word up to
the first space, in any case; a name may be in double quotes to hold spaces,
with @samp{\"} and @samp{\\} for a quote and a backslash in it.  Single quotes
mean nothing.  A relative name is the server's to place, in the user's
directory.

@table @code
@item chmod @var{mode} @var{file}
The mode is octal.
@item chown @var{uid} @var{file}
@itemx chgrp @var{gid} @var{file}
A number.  The other of the two is kept as it is.
@item mtime @var{date} @var{file}
@itemx atime @var{date} @var{file}
The date is one parameter, so in quotes: @samp{"Thu, 02 Jan 2020 10:00:00
GMT"}, with or without the day of the week, a zone as @samp{GMT} or
@samp{+0100} (none: local time), or as @code{asctime} writes it.  The other
time is kept as it is.
@item ln @var{source} @var{link}
@itemx symlink @var{source} @var{link}
Make @var{link} a symbolic link to @var{source}.
@item mkdir @var{dir}
@itemx rmdir @var{dir}
@itemx rm @var{file}
@itemx rename @var{from} @var{to}
As the names say.  @code{mkdir} makes a directory with mode 0755.
@item pwd
The server's current directory (its home), written to the header output
(@option{-D}, @option{-i}) as @samp{257 "/home/me" is current directory.}
@end table

@option{-I} runs the commands and looks at the file, and transfers nothing:
status 78 if it is not there.  That is a way to run commands alone.
@option{--ftp-create-dirs} makes the directories of the path of an upload
that are not there, from the top, and tries again; a directory that is there
is left, and if making them does not help the error is the upload's.

@subheading Rate limits

@option{--limit-rate} limits the average over the transfer, as curl's does.
A chunk is sent or asked for when the bytes before it have been earned at the
limit, so the transfer is ahead by at most one chunk.  A chunk is 32 KiB, or
half a second's worth of the limit when that is less, so a slow limit gives
small requests and not lumps; at 1000 bytes a second the requests are of 500
bytes.  The time spent waiting for a slow disk or a slow pipe is time, so
it counts.  The library takes @code{TWISTOPT_MAX_RECV_SPEED_LARGE} and
@code{TWISTOPT_MAX_SEND_SPEED_LARGE} for the two directions.

@subheading Logging in

A user name or password written in the URL is percent-encoded, as it must be
if it holds an @samp{@@}, a @samp{:} or a @samp{/}: @samp{p@@ss} is
@samp{p%40ss}.  twist uses what the encoding says.  This holds for every
scheme.  A name or password that decodes to a carriage return, a line feed or
a NUL is refused as a malformed URL, since it would otherwise reach a command
the protocol is built from.

Keys are tried in this order.  A key named with @option{--key} is used alone,
and nothing else is asked.  Otherwise the keys in an @command{ssh-agent}, in
the agent's order, then @file{~/.ssh/id_ed25519}, @file{id_ecdsa} and
@file{id_rsa}.  Then a password, from @option{-u} or the URL.

A server may ask for the password as a question (keyboard-interactive login,
usual with PAM) and not as a password.  twist answers when the server asks
one question that is not shown as it is typed, and the answer is the
password, as curl does.  Anything else, such as a verification code or
several questions, is answered with nothing, so the password is not given to
a question that might not be asking for it; the other ways are then tried, and
if they fail too the message says what was asked.  With no password, the
method is not tried.  A server that offers both a password and questions is
asked the question first, so a wrong password can count as two failed
attempts, as it does for @command{ssh}.

A key may have an OpenSSH user certificate, as @command{ssh} finds it: the
file of the key with @file{-cert.pub} added (@file{~/.ssh/id_ed25519-cert.pub},
or @file{key-cert.pub} for @option{--key key}).  The certificate is offered
before the key, which is still offered after it.  A certificate that cannot
be used is not offered, because the server only says that it refused: if the
login then fails the message says that the certificate has expired, is not yet
good, is not for the key, or does not name the user.  Certificates in an agent
need nothing from twist: @command{ssh-add} loads them with the key.  twist
does not check who signed a certificate; the server decides which CAs it
trusts.

The agent is the one named by the environment variable @env{SSH_AUTH_SOCK}.
twist asks it what it holds and to sign; the private keys never reach twist.
This includes keys that are protected by a passphrase, restricted to certain
servers (@command{ssh-add -h}), or kept on a security key.  Whatever is wrong
with the agent, whether there is none, it does not answer, or it refuses, is
not fatal: the other keys and the password are tried, and if they all fail
the message says what the agent did.  A server that counts every key it turns
away as a failed attempt, as @command{sshd} does, allows a limited number of
keys in the agent to be tried, six unless it is set otherwise.  This is the
server's limit, as it is for curl and @command{ssh}.

The key files twist reads are OpenSSH's, PEM and PKCS#8, and PuTTY's version
3.  @strong{A passphrase-protected key file in the OpenSSH format, which is
what @command{ssh-keygen} writes by default, cannot be read}, because its key
derivation is not in libgcrypt and twist does not take it from anywhere else.
Put the key in an agent (@command{ssh-add}), which can use it, or convert it
with @command{ssh-keygen -p -m PKCS8} and give its passphrase with
@option{--pass}.

The library takes the agent's socket from @code{TWISTOPT_SSH_AGENT_SOCKET}; an
empty string means no agent.

@subheading The host key

The host key is checked against @file{~/.ssh/known_hosts}, as the curl tool
does.  Here, and for the key files above, @file{~} is @env{HOME}, or the home
directory in the password file if @env{HOME} is not set or is empty.  A host that is not in the file, or whose key differs from the one in
it, fails the transfer (status 60); there is no way to accept a new host
key by asking.  Add it with @command{ssh-keyscan}, connect once with
@command{ssh}, or give its fingerprint with @option{--hostpubsha256}.
@option{-k} turns the check off.  A host known by one kind of key is asked
for that kind, so it cannot answer with another.

The library takes the same from @code{TWISTOPT_SSH_KNOWNHOSTS},
@code{TWISTOPT_SSH_HOST_PUBLIC_KEY_SHA256}, @code{TWISTOPT_SSLKEY} and
@code{TWISTOPT_KEYPASSWD}.

@subheading Several URLs

URLs for the same server on one command line are fetched on one connection:
the key exchange and the login happen once, and the SFTP channel stays open.
@code{sftp://} and @code{scp://} share it.  A connection is used again only
for the same host, port and user, and only when everything that decides who
you are or whom twist trusts is the same: the password, the key and its
passphrase, the known_hosts file, a pinned fingerprint, the agent, and whether
the host key is checked at all.  A session that was made with @option{-k}
is never used for a transfer that checks the host key.  A transfer that
fails closes its connection, and the next URL logs in again.  A connection
that is idle for two minutes, or that the server closes, is dropped.

With the library, the connections are kept in the multi handle (or the share)
that the transfers run on.  @code{twist_easy_perform} makes a multi handle of
its own for each call, so it keeps nothing for the next one.

@subheading Requirements

SSH comes from the foxtrot library, which needs libgcrypt 1.12.4 or later.

